public with sharing class ContactPhoneCleanup {

    /**
     * Normalizes the Phone field of the given contacts so it contains only
     * digits and '+' characters. Contacts without a phone are not updated.
     *
     * @param contactIds up to 200 contact Ids (bulk-safe: one query, one DML)
     * @return map of contact Id -> first error message for records that
     *         could not be saved; empty when all saves succeeded
     */
    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> errorsByContactId = new Map<Id, String>();

        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return errorsByContactId;
        }

        // Single SOQL for the whole batch (no queries in loops).
        List<Contact> contacts = [
            SELECT Id, Phone
            FROM Contact
            WHERE Id IN :contactIds
        ];

        List<Contact> toUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact c : contacts) {
            if (String.isNotBlank(c.Phone)) {
                // Strip everything that is not a digit or '+'.
                c.Phone = c.Phone.replaceAll('[^0-9+]', '');
                toUpdate.add(c);
            }
            // Contacts without a phone are left out of the update entirely.
        }

        if (toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            return errorsByContactId;
        }

        // allOrNone = false: a validation failure on one record does not block the rest.
        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.update(toUpdate, false);

        // Results are returned in the same order as the input records.
        // A failed save always reports at least one error, a successful save
        // reports none, so the error list determines success.
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            List<Database.Error> saveErrors = results[i].getErrors();
            if (saveErrors != null && !saveErrors.isEmpty()) {
                errorsByContactId.put(
                    toUpdate[i].Id,
                    saveErrors[0].getMessage()
                );
            }
        }

        return errorsByContactId;
    }
}
```

What changed: I replaced the `results[i].getSuccess()` call with a check of `results[i].getErrors()`. For every `Database.SaveResult` in a partial-success DML, a successful record has an empty error list and a failed record has at least one `Database.Error`, so the behavior is identical (failed records map to their first error message; succeeded records are omitted) — but it no longer depends on the method the compiler rejected. Everything else (single SOQL, single `Database.update(..., false)`, in-order result mapping, empty-map return) is unchanged.

