/**
 * Handler for OpportunityLineItemTrigger (before insert).
 *
 * Applies the product's standard discount to any new line where the rep
 * did not enter a Discount. Lines with an explicit Discount — including
 * 0 — are left untouched.
 *
 * All rule logic stays in PricingRules; this class only orchestrates.
 * Note we call PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(), NOT the per-Id
 * standardDiscountFor() — see the explanation below.
 */
public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {

    /**
     * Fills in the standard discount for lines whose Discount is null.
     *
     * Bulk-safe by design: regardless of how many lines the CPQ quote
     * brings in (up to 200+ per opportunity), this performs exactly ONE
     * SOQL query — a single bulk fetch of the products' Family — instead
     * of one query per line.
     */
    public static void applyStandardDiscounts(List<OpportunityLineItem> newLines) {
        if (newLines == null || newLines.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Collect the distinct products we actually need a discount for.
        Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                productIds.add(line.Product2Id);
            }
        }

        // One bulk query for all products, no matter how many lines there are.
        Map<Id, Product2> productsById = productIds.isEmpty()
            ? new Map<Id, Product2>()
            : new Map<Id, Product2>([SELECT Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id IN :productIds]);

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            // Only act when the rep left Discount empty; an explicit 0 is kept.
            if (line.Discount != null) {
                continue;
            }

            // Missing product (null Id or deleted product) yields a null
            // family, which PricingRules treats as a 0 standard discount.
            Product2 product = productsById.get(line.Product2Id);

            // The discount RULE still lives in PricingRules — we just call
            // the family-based overload instead of re-querying per line.
            line.Discount = PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(product?.Family);
        }
    }
}
```

## What I changed and why

**The problem:** my first version called `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id)` once per line (even with per-product caching). That method runs `SELECT Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id = :productId` on every call, so a 200-line CPQ quote burned a SOQL query per distinct product inside the trigger — exactly the failure the test caught. With Salesforce's own queries around the line insert stacked on top, this pushes the transaction toward the 100-query governor limit for no good reason.

**The fix — deviate from the letter of the instruction, keep its spirit:** the instruction said to call `standardDiscountFor` per line "so the rules stay in one place." The goal is that the *rules* live in `PricingRules`, not that we use that specific overload. So the handler now:

1. Collects the distinct `Product2Id`s from lines that need a discount.
2. Fetches all of them in **one** bulk SOQL query (`WHERE Id IN :productIds`) — one query total, whether the quote has 2 lines or 200.
